A||||2||mbramble||Long But Fruitful Weekend (long)||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||10-01-2000||07:59 PM||||Friday, I ordered a dogbone, motor mount and tranny mount for my '87 very basic Duke coupe.<P>When I got home from work, and my son got the car home from school, we yanked off the coupe fascia and installed an aero fascia off an '85 GT. That was a lot of fun... One question here -- there is a very large open space below in front of the radiator.  Is it supposed to be that way or is there some part missing here??  Seems as though the radiator is vulnerable to rocks, etc, coming up thru there.<P>Sat, spent all day installing the wiring harness for power windows, locks, and mirrors.  Quite surprised when I got over to the driver's side and had a couple of leftover plugs!!  I think they may have been for courtesy lamps or something that my car doesn't have.  So, just tucked them out of the way and went on about my business.  When I had the harness run and connected to power, pulled over the passenger door, propped it up and plugged it in.  Everything worked.  Doors will probably be installed next weekend.  Put in a replacement dash and the rear section of the console.<P>Sunday, spent all day installing a sunroof and putting the car back together so my son can drive it to school tomorrow.  I also recovered the headliner.  Had a small problem there.  Having never seen a completely installed Fiero sunroof, I didn't know there was a trim strip that ran around the inside that you are supposed to tuck the headliner into!!  I went around merrily folding it over the h/l board like I did around the outside edges of the board.  Then it dawned on me what I was supposed to have done!!  Ended up with a couple 3'4 " cuts showing on the front corners of the trim area.  I put some scrap material behind them so it doesn't look too bad.<P>Also installed the switch and relay for the remote deck lid opener but didn't get the trunk parts mounted yet.  <P>Also yanked out the 100x4 amp I had mounted in the front storage area because I could never get rid of the interference from the ECM, etc.  Rewired my stereo like stock and mounted the FM modulator for my CD player down in the area where the premium sound system amp would be.  STill have the CD mounted up front.<P>So, from my basic coupe on Fri morning I now have an aero fascia, sun roof, power doors, locks, and mirrors (once I put the doors on) and a remote deck lid opener (once I finish installing the other parts).  Pretty cool!!<P>I am now looking for the ground effects pieces for the front fenders and for the lower rear fenders.  If anyone has those, I sure am interested.  The new doors have FX on them and I am thinking of keeping them.<P>The other things I am planning to do, besides paint after everything is done are side scoops, George's hood scoop, and Toms' rear deck scoop, and rebuild my Duke.  I have no interest in a 6 or 8 cylinder.  I love the Duke -- reasonable power, and great gas mileage!! I am 45 and though I tend to go above the posted limits on highways occassionally, I don't race and I'm not too concerned about how long it takes me to get to those speeds.<P>Would appreciate if someone could point out to me what may be missing under the aero fascia leaving that large opening beneath (as compared to my coupe fascia espacially) and would like to hear from anyone with the ground fx pieces I am looking for.<P>It was a great weekend and I am tired as he!!.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||10-01-2000||09:04 PM||||Wow! Sounds like you had your hands full this weekend! Great job though.<BR>Im not quite sure Im understanding you correctly on the open space below/in front of the radiator. If its UNDER the car, behind the front facia, then there is supposed to be a black "shielding" of sorts that goes from the end of the facia (on the under side) to pretty much under the radiator...this is the protection for the radiator. Its screwed in..that sounds like the piece you're missing.<BR>Now Ive got a question for you...my Formula is pretty much a plain janer herself..manual windows,locks,and mirrors. How difficult was it in installing these power components? Thanx, Brian||||reg||14
Z||000002||mbramble||10-01-2000||10:42 PM||||Technically speaking, it wasn't too difficult.  The hard part is the disassembly/reassembly of the inside of the car.  If you are only doing these options you don't have to get as carried away as I did.  But, I knew I was replacing the headliner, console, etc., so I basically gutted the interior.  Ogre has good writeups in his 'Cave' about adding power windows and power deck lid.  Start from the wiring that goes into the door and pull the connectors and the harness they are in back thru the dash, to the switches to the other side of the dash and the other door.  I pulled two harnesses; one was quite simple for the power mirrors, ther other was for the door locks and windows.  It obviously was for something else as well!!  I started installing from the passenger side and replaced my door 'pin' switch with the one from the bundle, left the passenger door connectors in place and started putting the harness into place, bringing the wires down to the center console for the switches.  When I got to the driver's side is when I got in a little panic!! There were two connectors (besides the one that go into the door, that didn't mate with anything in my car....  I decided they must be for courtesy lightts and or other options that I don't have.  Since I didn't pull the mating connectors from the donor, I just tied them back out of the way.  I went back to the passenger side and reconnected the door pin switch back to my harness.  So, though there were obviously other functions in that donor harness, that wouldn't mate to my harness, I figured it would be simple enough to just leave those extra connectors alone and not disconnect anything from my original harness so as not to kill anything like the dome ligts coming on, etc.  The only real quandry (once I decided all this!!) was that there was a connector that needed to go to the power block (orange white wires) and there was no other place to plug it in.  In retospect, this was probably for the courtesy lights or whatever else was involved in that harness but I didn't want to have to go back and add power later.  So, I just spliced the orange & white wires to a set coming off the power block.  I taped up the two leftover connectors and put them out of the way.  This is when I pulled over a door for testing.  <P>The power windows were very simple, a ground on the passenger side and a plug for that door, the switch unit, a power plug on the driver side that plugged right in to the power block and a plug for the driver's door.<P>The lock/window harness also had some sort of relay can that mounted to the passenger side frame.  I think! that may be a relay for the power locks.  Not sure though.  <P>So, read Ogre's notes and pay closer attention to what you pull out than I did!!  If I'd known that those two connectors would not mate to my car, I would probably have gone ahead and pull their matching plugs and harnesses and had who knows what kind of new options!!<P>Thanks for the info on the black piece for under the fascia.  I'm sure that is what I'm missing.  There was not one on the donor or I would have taken it.  Guess I can find something to put under there!!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 10-02-2000).]||||reg||1
